@page "/profiles"

<PageTitle>Draft vs Submit</PageTitle>
<h1>Draft vs Submit profiles</h1>
<p>Two profiles, one validator: <em>Save draft</em> validates leniently (format rules only), <em>Submit</em> validates for completeness. Typing follows the submit side — a field you have engaged says whatever would block a submit, while a field you have never touched stays quiet however badly its rule is failing.</p>

<TeachingPanel CodeFiles="@(new[] { "DraftedBrief.cs", "Profiles.razor", "Profiles.razor.cs" })">
    <Rules>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Title</strong>: 60 characters max. A format rule in the Draft profile: enforced always, including while drafting.</li>
            <li><strong>Title</strong>: required. A completeness rule in the Submit profile: a draft save ignores it, and engaging the field is what puts it on screen.</li>
            <li><strong>Summary</strong>: required. Its own completeness rule in the same profile, disclosed the same way: silent until you engage the field or press Submit.</li>
            <li><strong>Reset</strong>: calls <code>_form.ResetAsync()</code> — a named verb for returning the SAME model instance to pristine: touched/modified state, messages and <code>HasSubmitted</code> all clear, without swapping to a different object.</li>
        </ul>
    </Rules>
    <TryIt>
        <ol>
            <li>Type more than 60 characters into Title — the format error appears as you leave the field.</li>
            <li>Click <em>Save draft</em> — blocked: format rules run even for drafts.</li>
            <li>Shorten the title and <em>Save draft</em> again — it saves, even though Summary is empty.</li>
            <li>Now clear Title altogether and leave the field — "Title is required to submit" appears at once, with no submit anywhere: the live channel runs the submit profile's rules, and Title is a field you have engaged. Summary is failing its own required rule and says nothing, because you have never touched it.</li>
            <li>Click <em>Submit</em> — Summary speaks for the first time. Title's entry was already in the error summary from the step before, so the submit is what adds the second one.</li>
            <li>Click any entry in the error summary — it focuses the offending field.</li>
            <li>Fill everything in and submit — the status line below confirms it.</li>
            <li>Type a 61-character title again and leave the field, so the format error is showing before you touch anything else, then click <em>Reset</em> — the error clears and the form returns to pristine, but the over-long title stays in the box: <code>ResetAsync()</code> never writes model properties.</li>
        </ol>
    </TryIt>
</TeachingPanel>

<FormidableForm @ref="_form" Model="_brief" OnValidSubmit="HandleValid">
    <div class="summary-slot">
        <FormidableSummary />
    </div>

    <div class="field"><label>Title <FormidableInputText @bind-Value="_brief.Title" /></label>
        <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _brief.Title" /></div>
    <div class="field"><label>Summary <FormidableInputText @bind-Value="_brief.Summary" /></label>
        <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _brief.Summary" /></div>

    <div class="actions">
        <button type="submit">Submit</button>
        <button type="button" @onclick="SaveDraft">Save draft</button>
        <button type="button" @onclick="Reset">Reset</button>
    </div>
</FormidableForm>

<p role="status">@_status</p>
